{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Behold, I am coming, etc.</b> Meaning: I agree to come on this mission, and there is no place for the objections I raised; only, it is first necessary to be believed as a prophet to Israel, and I must come to them beforehand. <b>And they will say to me, What is His name?</b> — meaning, it is straightforward to me that they will say to me, What is His name? And the reason is that you will find that in every prophecy of promises to the patriarchs, HaShem mentions His name to them. To Abraham He said in the portion of לך לך (Genesis 15:7): ״אֲנִי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ״ etc., and in the prophecy of the promise of seed and land He further said to him (Genesis 17:1): ״אֲנִי אֵל שַׁדַּי״, and likewise to Jacob He said in the portion of ויצא (Genesis 28:13): ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֲנִי ה׳״, and further in the portion of וישלח He said to him (Genesis 35:11): ״אֲנִי אֵל שַׁדַּי״ etc. From this you have learned that in all the promises He mentions the name of the One who promises, blessed be He, and all the more so when He sends a prophet to another, that he must state the name of the sender. Or perhaps it is that in every prophecy that HaShem prophesies to His servants He recalls His name, and one may infer the implicit from the explicit. And since when HaShem spoke to Moses in this prophecy He did not mention His name to him, but only said to him: ״אָנֹכִי אֱלֹהֵי אָבִיךָ״ etc., therefore he said to Him: <b>Behold, I am coming to the children of Israel and I will say to them, the Elohim of your fathers</b>, as HaShem said to Moses, <b>I am the Elohim of your father, the Elohim of Abraham</b>, without mentioning the Name — <b>and they will say to me, What is His name?</b> — meaning, because I do not mention the Name in the recollection, they will ask." "And with this the mind finds rest regarding one investigation: what sign and wonder lies in knowing the Name or in mentioning it? For regardless: if this name is known to the children of Israel, there is no sign of good in it, for just as Israel knew it, Moses too knew it, and there is no proof; and if the Name is not known — for this is His name, blessed be He, until now — and who would believe him that this is an awesome name? And I have seen among the commentators that they gave far-fetched explanations and ways, but whoever has a palate to taste will taste our words and they will be sweet to his mouth, for it is not for a sign or wonder that he desires the mention of the Name. And you will find that HaShem told him the name ״אֶהְיֶה״, even though we do not find that it was mentioned in any of the prophecies to the patriarchs, and presumably it would not be known to the children except the names that were made known to the patriarchs — rather, surely there is no sign or wonder in the mention of the Name, but its reason is as we have written. And this is the beginning of recognizing Moses as faithfully trustworthy in all his missions and prophecies, for he scrutinized the words of HaShem that He did not mention the Name, and he did not rely on his own judgment to insert it on his own and mention His name once the faith of prophecy had been grasped by him; and presumably it was certainly known to Moses that there was one name among the names of the One prophesying. Further, it is possible that Moses intended to gain knowledge of what is the attribute through which HaShem speaks with him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:08.362898701Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}